#import "style.typ": *
#chapter[Galois cohomology and cyclotomic towers]

== Measuring an extension <c06s01>

Let a group $G$ act on an abelian group $M$. A one-cocycle is a function $c$ satisfying $c(g h)=c(g)+g c(h)$. A coboundary has the form $c(g)=g m-m$. The quotient of cocycles by coboundaries is $H^(1)(G,M)$. For a profinite Galois group all cochains carry the required continuity. If the action is trivial, $H^1$ is the group of continuous homomorphisms. For instance $H^(1)(ℤ slash 2ℤ,ℤ slash 2ℤ)$ has two elements with trivial action.

In linear algebra the rule is visible in the upper-right corner of a triangular representation. The product of matrices with diagonal characters $alpha,beta$ forces the off-diagonal entry, after dividing by $beta$, to be a cocycle for $alpha beta^(-1)$. Changing a choice of lift in the extension changes it by a coboundary. One can therefore read a nontrivial class either as a cocycle or as the failure of a short exact sequence to split equivariantly. Restricting to a decomposition group tests whether it splits locally at that place.

Higher cohomology enters because a short exact sequence of modules gives a long exact cohomology sequence. In particular, $H^2$ contains obstructions to lifting $H^1$ classes. For finite modules over $ℤ_ℓ$, composition length is additive in an exact sequence. A group $ℤ slash ℓ^3 ℤ$ has length three and cardinality $ℓ^3$. Consequently a local length multiplied by $log ℓ$ counts a logarithmic size. A sum of such lengths over rational primes can be compared with a global covolume.

== What continuity requires of a Galois cocycle <c06s02>

The absolute Galois group $G_K$ is the inverse limit of the Galois groups of the finite Galois extensions inside a separable closure of $K$. It is compact, and the open normal subgroups are kernels of projections to finite quotients. A continuous map from $G_K$ to a finite discrete set is locally constant. There is then a single open normal subgroup on whose cosets its values are constant. To construct the subgroup, cover the domain by finitely many open cosets on which it is constant and refine them by a common open normal subgroup. This applies to the action on a finite Galois module $M$, and separately to a continuous cocycle into $M$. Thus a finite-coefficient class can be represented on a finite Galois quotient, although finding the right quotient can already contain substantial arithmetic information.

For discrete infinite torsion coefficients a continuous map still has finite image, by compactness. For compact $ℤ_ℓ$-coefficients a different principle applies. The compatible reductions of a representation modulo $ℓ^n$ can all have finite image even though the original representation has infinite image. Taking an inverse limit of finite cohomology groups requires control of the corresponding inverse system and hypotheses permitting the relevant kernels and cokernels to pass to the limit. This distinction explains why the arithmetic duality formulas used in the proof are stated for particular compact and discrete modules rather than obtained by an informal substitution of $n=infinity$.

Let $G$ act on an additive module $M$. A one-cocycle $c$ is determined in a cyclic example by a single value. If $G$ is generated by $sigma$ of order $d$, put $N=1+sigma+dots+sigma^(d-1)$ in its group ring. Writing $m=c(sigma)$, repeated use of the cocycle equation gives
$ c(sigma^r)=(1+sigma+dots+sigma^(r-1))m. $
The relation $sigma^d=1$ forces $N m=0$, and every $m$ satisfying this condition defines a cocycle. A coboundary changes $m$ by $(sigma-1)b$. Consequently
$ H^(1)(G,M)=ker(N) slash (sigma-1)M. $
For trivial action on $ℤ slash n ℤ$ this consists of the elements killed by $d$, a cyclic group of order $gcd(d,n)$. For $d=2$ acting by sign on $ℤ$, the norm is zero and $sigma-1$ is multiplication by $-2$. The result is $ℤ slash 2ℤ$. The module is torsion-free before taking cohomology, yet its first cohomology contains torsion.

The degree-two cohomology of the cyclic group is
$ H^(2)(G,M)=M^G slash N M. $
It follows by applying the periodic resolution of the cyclic group, whose successive maps are alternately $sigma-1$ and $N$. To check exactness of that resolution in the integral group ring, write $b=sum_(j=0)^(d-1) b_j sigma^j$. The kernel of $sigma-1$ consists of multiples of $N$, since all coefficients must be equal. Multiplication by $N$ returns $(sum_j b_j)N$, whose kernel consists of differences $(sigma-1)a$ by taking successive partial sums of the $b_j$. The augmentation is the sum of coefficients, and has the same difference kernel. Applying homomorphisms from the resolution to $M$ gives the two formulas. They are concrete instances of cohomology built from an exact sequence, and already distinguish a restriction on a cocycle from a class modulo norms.

== Triangular representations and their local restrictions <c06s03>

Let $alpha,beta$ be one-dimensional characters of a group over a field. A two-dimensional representation with those characters on a subspace and on its quotient can be written
$ R(g)=mat(alpha(g),u(g);0,beta(g)). $
The homomorphism identity gives $u(g h)=alpha(g)u(h)+u(g)beta(h)$. Dividing by $beta(g h)$ shows that $c(g)=frac(u(g),beta(g))$ satisfies the cocycle identity for the action by the ratio $alpha beta^(-1)$. To change a lift of a basis vector in the quotient, add $b$ times the basis vector in the subspace. Then the new off-diagonal entry is $u(g)+b(alpha(g)-beta(g))$. Thus the new cocycle differs by $g b-b$. A simultaneous choice of lifts which diagonalizes the whole representation exists precisely when the class of $c$ is zero.

Take, for example, the cyclic additive group $ℤ slash ℓ ℤ$ over the coefficient field $𝔽_ℓ$. The matrices $mat(1,r;0,1)$ for residues $r$ give an extension of the trivial character by itself. It is a representation because upper entries add. Its cocycle $r mapsto r$ is a nontrivial homomorphism. With trivial action every additive coboundary vanishes, so no change of lifts splits this extension. Over characteristic zero the corresponding finite cyclic group has no nonzero homomorphism into an additive characteristic-zero field. The obstruction is therefore a genuine feature of integral or residual coefficients. A comparison of characteristic-zero representations alone cannot count it.

For a subgroup $H$ one restricts a cocycle by evaluating only on $H$. A class can restrict to zero even when it is nonzero globally. More generally different subgroups can allow different changes of lift, so that compatibility of local splittings is a nontrivial global question. If $H$ is normal, one can locate the first such ambiguity. Let $G'=G slash H$ and $M^H$ be the elements fixed by $H$. A cocycle of $G'$ with values in $M^H$ pulls back to one on $G$. It restricts to zero on $H$. Conversely a class whose restriction to $H$ is zero can be represented, after subtracting a single coboundary on $G$, by a cocycle $c$ with $c(h)=0$ on $H$. Then $c(g h)=c(g)$ and $c(h g)=h c(g)$. Since $h g=g(g^(-1)h g)$, its value also equals $c(g)$. Thus $c(g)$ is fixed by $H$ and depends only on the coset of $g$. The resulting quotient cocycle is defined up to a coboundary in $M^H$. This proves exactness at the first two arrows in the inflation and restriction sequence
$ 0 arrow.r H^(1)(G',M^H) arrow.r H^(1)(G,M) arrow.r H^(1)(H,M). $

For a number field a decomposition subgroup at a place acts on the completion. Restricting a global representation to that subgroup supplies its local representation. Testing a class at several places intersects the corresponding kernels. For instance with trivial coefficients $𝔽_2$ and $G=(ℤ slash 2ℤ)^2$, the restrictions to the two coordinate lines together detect every homomorphism $G arrow.r 𝔽_2$. Either restriction alone has a one-dimensional kernel. The arithmetic group used later tests a prescribed collection of places with nontrivial coefficients and a global ramification condition. Its dimension and length can vary with the field and the twist.

== A connecting homomorphism one can compute <c06s04>

For a short exact sequence $0 arrow.r A arrow.r B arrow.r C arrow.r 0$ of $G$-modules, an element $c in C^G$ may lack a $G$-fixed lift in $B$. Choose a lift $b$. For every $g$ the difference $g b-b$ lies in $A$, and the map $g mapsto g b-b$ is an $A$-valued cocycle. Changing $b$ by an element of $A$ changes it by a coboundary. Its class is zero exactly when a fixed lift exists. This constructs the connecting map $C^G arrow.r H^(1)(G,A)$ and proves exactness there. To obtain the degree-two obstruction, lift the values of a one-cocycle to $B$ and measure the failure of the cocycle identity. The failures give an $A$-valued two-cocycle. Vanishing of its class lets one correct the lifts.

For an integer $n>=1$, take a field $K$ of characteristic zero, its algebraic closure $overline(K)$, and the multiplicative power map $x mapsto x^n$. Its kernel is $mu_n$, the $n$th roots of unity, with their actual Galois action. For $a in K^times$ choose $b$ with $b^n=a$. The element $frac(g(b),b)$ then lies in $mu_n$ and gives a multiplicative cocycle. Changing $b$ by a root of unity changes this cocycle by a coboundary. Changing $a$ by an $n$th power in $K$ leaves the class unchanged. Conversely the class is trivial exactly when one can choose $b$ in $K$.

The fact that all $mu_n$-valued classes arise in this fashion is Hilbert's Theorem 90 for the multiplicative group. A short proof for a finite Galois extension with group $G$ is instructive. If $(c_g)$ is a multiplicative cocycle, choose $a$ for which $b=sum_(g in G)c_g g(a)$ is nonzero. Such an $a$ exists by linear independence of the distinct field automorphisms as functions. The cocycle identity gives $h(b)=c_h^(-1)b$, so $c_h=frac(h(b^(-1)),b^(-1))$. The same conclusion for continuous cocycles on the absolute group follows after passing to a finite Galois extension containing their finitely many values and large enough for their maps to factor. In particular, if all values lie in $mu_n$ and $c_g=frac(g(t),t)$, then $t^n$ is fixed by every $g$. This proves
$ K^times slash (K^times)^n ≃ H^(1)(G_K,mu_n). $
The field need not itself contain all the $n$th roots of unity. Removing the Galois action on $mu_n$ when it is present would change the right side.

For $K=ℚ$ and $n=2$, the class of $-1$ describes the quadratic extension $ℚ(i)$. Its restriction to $ℚ_5$ is zero because a root of $X^2+1$ lifts from the residue field, as computed in Chapter 4. At $ℚ_3$ no such root exists. At the real place the class is again nonzero. This elementary example shows what a local test of a global class means. It does not identify the exact restriction kernel in the proof with square classes. That kernel has a specified negative cyclotomic twist and different ramification conditions.

== Twists and a local restriction condition <c06s05>

The action on the compatible system of $ℓ$-power roots of unity gives the cyclotomic character $kappa_ℓ$ with values in $ℤ_ℓ^times$. The twist $M(j)$ multiplies the action by $kappa_ℓ^j$. For a number field $E$ let $S$ be the places above $ℓ$ and let $G_(E,S)$ be the Galois group of the maximal extension unramified at finite places outside $S$. A group that occurs in the proof is
$ ker( H^(1)(G_(E,S),(ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ)(-j))
    arrow.r product_(v divides ℓ) H^(1)(E_v,(ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ)(-j)) ). $
Its elements are globally defined classes which restrict to zero at the indicated places. We denote it by $Sha_(ℓ)(E,j)$. The symbol refers to this precise restriction kernel. It is useful to view it as the residual ambiguity left after imposing compatible local conditions.

For a finite family of twists, showing that these groups are finite and bounding their total logarithmic size are different tasks. In the theta calculation the twist index varies with the scale. A theorem for each fixed twist, with an uncontrolled constant depending on that twist, would not bound their sum. The cohomological window theorem is structured to supply exactly that uniformity.

== The inertia of a twist and the length of its invariants <c06s06>

Choose compatible primitive $ℓ^n$th roots of unity in an algebraic closure. A Galois element acts on each by an exponent prime to $ℓ$, compatibly in $n$. The inverse limit of those exponents is the cyclotomic character $kappa_ℓ$ with values in $ℤ_ℓ^times$. The resulting rank-one compact module is $ℤ_(ℓ)(1)$. Tensor powers give positive integral twists and the dual gives the negative twists. A coefficient module $M(j)$ carries the original action multiplied by $kappa_ℓ^j$. At a place above a rational prime different from $ℓ$, where unramified arithmetic Frobenius is defined, the action of that Frobenius on roots of unity of $ℓ$-power order is the residue norm. Its action on the $j$th Tate twist is therefore the $j$th power of that norm.

To compute invariants in the discrete module $M_j=(ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ)(-j)$, an element of exact order $ℓ^r$ is fixed precisely when every $g$ satisfies $kappa_(ℓ)(g)^j equiv 1$ modulo $ℓ^r$. Suppose some $g$ has $kappa_(ℓ)(g)^j!=1$ and put $s=min_g v_(ℓ)(kappa_(ℓ)(g)^j-1)$, ignoring zero differences in taking the minimum. Then the invariant subgroup is exactly the subgroup killed by $ℓ^s$. It has length $s$ over $ℤ_ℓ$, including the possibility $s=0$. For coefficients killed by $ℓ^n$ the corresponding length is $min(n,s)$. This is an elementary calculation about fixed elements, independent of any global duality theorem.

#example[Two different invariant lengths at five][For $K=ℚ_5$ the cyclotomic character has image $ℤ_5^times$, by the local cyclotomic extension theorem. Reduction modulo five has order four. If $4 nmid j$, one can find a unit whose $j$th power already differs from one modulo five, so the invariant length is zero. If $4$ divides $j>0$, write a unit as a fourth root of unity times a principal unit. For the generator $1+5$ of the principal units one has $v_(5)((1+5)^j-1)=1+v_(5)(j)$. Every principal unit has valuation at least this high after taking its $j$th power and subtracting one. Hence the length equals one at $j=4$, equals two at $j=20$, and equals zero at $j=2$. The finite torsion factor and the procyclic factor have different effects.]

For odd $ℓ$ the valuation formula underlying this example has a direct proof. If $u=1+ℓ^a b$ with $a>=1$ and $b$ a unit, binomial expansion gives $v_(ℓ)(u^m-1)=a$ for $m$ prime to $ℓ$, and $v_(ℓ)(u^ℓ-1)=a+1$. In the second calculation the linear term has valuation $a+1$, every intermediate binomial coefficient is divisible by $ℓ$ and multiplies a term of valuation at least $2a$, and the last term has valuation $ℓ a>a+1$. Taking successive powers yields $v_(ℓ)(u^j-1)=a+v_(ℓ)(j)$. At $ℓ=2$ the same proof works when $a>=2$, since the last term then has valuation strictly bigger than $a+1$. The sign factor in $ℤ_2^times$ has to be handled separately. Such distinctions reappear when one tries to bound many twists uniformly.

== A local class at a prime away from the coefficient prime <c06s07>

Let $v$ be a finite place with residue field of size $q$, of characteristic different from $ℓ$. Suppose $A$ is a finite $ℓ$-primary module on which inertia acts trivially. A local cohomology class is called unramified when its restriction to inertia vanishes. The finite-module version of the inflation and restriction argument gives
$ H^1_("unr")(E_v,A) ≃ H^(1)(G_v slash I_v,A) ≃ A slash (F-1)A, $
where $F$ is arithmetic Frobenius. For the second isomorphism the quotient is the profinite cyclic group generated topologically by $F$. A continuous cocycle is determined by its value $m$ at $F$. Conversely every $m in A$ occurs. Indeed choose a positive integer $r$ killing the action of $F$ in the sense that $F^r$ acts identically, and a positive integer $b$ annihilating the additive group $A$. The sum of $F^j m$ for $0<=j<r b$ is $b$ times the sum over $0<=j<r$, hence zero. The cyclic cocycle with initial value $m$ therefore factors through the quotient of order $r b$. As before, coboundaries change $m$ by $(F-1)A$.

If $A=(ℤ slash ℓ^n ℤ)(-j)$ for $j>=1$, Frobenius acts by $q^(-j)$ modulo $ℓ^n$. Its difference from one has the same valuation as $q^j-1$ because $q$ is a unit. Thus the unramified degree-one group has length $min(n,v_(ℓ)(q^j-1))$. For $q=7$ and $ℓ^n=9$, at $j=1$ the eigenvalue modulo nine is four, the difference is three, and the quotient has three elements. At $j=3$ the eigenvalue is one modulo nine, and the quotient has nine elements. This computation determines the unramified subgroup. Allowing ramification at the same place can give further classes.

The formula distinguishes two kinds of local input in the global argument. Outside the chosen ramification set, a representation has a prescribed unramified behavior and its Frobenius eigenvalues carry arithmetic information. At the places above $ℓ$, the restriction kernel in Chapter 15 imposes vanishing of an entire localized class, while a de Rham or crystalline condition on a representation involves further structure. A single residue-field eigenvalue cannot encode all those conditions. Keeping the place $v$, its residue characteristic, and the coefficient prime separate avoids transporting an unramified calculation into the wrong part of the proof.

== Duality converts a global kernel into a length identity <c06s08>

The dual of a finite $ℓ$-power torsion Galois module $A$ for local arithmetic duality is $A^(∨)(1)=op("Hom")(A,mu_(ℓ^infinity))$, with its natural action. The twist by one reflects the target of the cup product. Local Tate duality at a nonarchimedean completion gives a perfect pairing of finite groups
$ H^(i)(K_v,A) times H^(2-i)(K_v,A^(∨)(1)) arrow.r ℚ slash ℤ. $
The relevant component of the target is its $ℓ$-primary part. For finite coefficients $A=(ℤ slash ℓ^n ℤ)(j+1)$ its paired module is $(ℤ slash ℓ^n ℤ)(-j)$. The exponents add to one. Thus the invariants of the negative twist pair with degree-two cohomology of the positive twist. At a complex place the absolute Galois group is trivial and the modified archimedean terms of the global duality sequence vanish there. The totally imaginary field hypothesis simplifies the formula used later. #link("https://www.jmilne.org/math/Books/ADTnot.pdf")[Milne, Arithmetic Duality Theorems, Part I.]

Take $T_j=ℤ_(ℓ)(j+1)$ and its discrete Tate dual $M_j=(ℚ_ℓ slash ℤ_ℓ)(-j)$. These are the two coefficient modules introduced in #link(<c06s05>)[§6.5]. In the finite-length situation of Chapter 15 put $B=H^(2)(G_(E,S),T_j)$ and $C=⨁_(v divides ℓ) H^(2)(E_v,T_j)$. Denote the localization map by $b:B arrow.r C$. For any such homomorphism,
$ len B+len(op("coker")(b))=len C+len(ker b). $
This follows by applying additivity to the short exact sequences with middle terms $B$ and $C$ and cancelling the common image length. Local duality gives $len C=sum_(v divides ℓ)len H^(0)(E_v,M_j)$. The Poitou–Tate comparison identifies the length of the localization kernel with the length of the degree-one restriction kernel for $M_j$, and the cokernel length with the length of global invariants. Write $h(F)=len H^(0)(F,M_j)$ for the invariant length over a global or local field. Then
$ len B+h(E)=len Sha_(ℓ)(E,j)+sum_(v divides ℓ) h(E_v). $
The global identifications and their compact/discrete limit hypotheses are arithmetic theorems. The signs in this equality come only from the two elementary exact sequences once the identifications have been made.

Here $Sha_(ℓ)(E,j)$ denotes the degree-one kernel defined earlier in the chapter, over the same set of allowed ramified places. An upper estimate for $len B$ yields an estimate for that kernel once the invariant terms have been handled. A local invariant that persists over many twists can matter in the sum even if each individual length is small. The weight $log ℓ$ that appears when these estimates are summed over primes converts length into logarithmic order, since a finite $ℤ_ℓ$-module of length $a$ has $ℓ^a$ elements. The logarithm of the product of the orders of independent primary factors is their sum of lengths weighted by $log ℓ$.

== When Fourier projection preserves an integral lattice <c06s09>

Suppose a finite abelian group $H$ acts on a lattice $P$ over a discrete valuation ring $O$ of residue characteristic $ℓ$. Assume its fraction field has characteristic zero and contains the values of the characters in question. If $|H|$ is prime to $ℓ$, the orthogonal projectors of Chapter 4 act integrally. Indeed the formula
$ e_chi=frac(1,|H|)sum_(h in H)chi(h)^(-1)h $
has coefficients in $O$ because the roots of unity and the denominator are all units. Multiplying two such expressions and applying orthogonality proves $e_chi e_eta=0$ for distinct characters and $e_chi^2=e_chi$. Summing them gives the identity if all the characters are present. The identities hold in $O[H]$ because its map into the fraction-field group ring is injective. Consequently the lattice, and every finite quotient with a compatible $H$-action, splits into these character parts. Its total length is the sum of their lengths.

At $ℓ$ dividing the group order that argument can discard a congruence. Consider $H$ cyclic of order three and $P=ℤ_3[H]$ with its regular action. The invariant vectors are multiples of $N=1+h+h^2$. Coinvariants are obtained by imposing $h=1$, so the natural map from invariants to coinvariants sends $N$ to three. Over $ℚ_3$ this map is invertible on the invariant line, but over $ℤ_3$ it has cokernel of length one. The rational projector onto invariants would divide $N$ by three and send the integral vector one outside $P$. A characteristic-zero decomposition therefore misses one power of three in this comparison.

After reducing the example modulo three, put $t=h-1$. Then $𝔽_3[H]=𝔽_3[t] slash (t^3)$ and $N=t^2$. The action has a filtration
$ 0 subset t^2 P_0 subset t P_0 subset P_0, quad P_0=𝔽_3[H], $
with each successive quotient a one-dimensional trivial character. There are three trivial composition factors and just one line of invariant vectors. The extension between the factors is visible in multiplication by $t$. Counting residual character dimensions in a semisimplification does not recover this extension information. The cohomological estimates later in the book distinguish prime-to-$ℓ$ characters from the residual $ℓ$-part precisely to retain the integral information.

Complex conjugation gives another important projection. If $c^2=1$ and $ℓ$ is odd, the two operators $frac(1+c,2)$ and $frac(1-c,2)$ split any $ℓ$-primary module into plus and minus eigenspaces. At two take $c$ exchanging the coordinates of $ℤ_2^2$. The plus and minus lines are generated by $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$. Their spans have trivial intersection but their sum has index two, by taking the determinant of the pair. A proof with uniform integral bounds at all primes therefore singles out the two-primary tower. Its exceptional treatment has a visible algebraic cause already in this rank-two example.

There is also a normalization when adjoining character values to the coefficient field. If $O$ is the ring of integers in a finite extension of $ℚ_ℓ$ with ramification index $e$ and residue degree $f$, then $ℓ O$ is generated by a unit times $varpi^e$. For a finite $ℤ_ℓ$-module $A$ of length $r$ one has
$ len_(O)(A ⊗ O)=e r. $
Decompose $A$ into cyclic modules, or tensor a composition series with the finite free module $O$. Each residue-field piece becomes $O slash ℓ O$, of length $e$. The residue field of $O$ has cardinality $ℓ^f$. Dividing the logarithmic order after scalar extension by $[op("Frac")(O):ℚ_ℓ]=e f$ recovers $r log ℓ$. Thus scalar extension used to split tame characters can preserve a *normalized logarithmic size* while changing ordinary coefficient length. A proof comparing many field realizations has to specify which size it sums.

== Why a tower helps <c06s10>

Adjoining higher $ℓ$-power roots of unity produces a tower with a procyclic direction. A topological generator acts by an operator often written $1+T$, allowing compatible modules in the tower to be studied over the power series ring $ℤ_ℓ[[T]]$. Specializing $T$ turns a power series into an $ℓ$-adic number. Near an exceptional specialization this number can be unusually divisible, even if the power series is fixed.

#example[A single exceptional specialization][For odd $ℓ$ take $P(T)=T-ℓ$. Evaluating at $T=(1+ℓ)^j-1$ gives $(1+ℓ)((1+ℓ)^(j-1)-1)$. If $j!=1$, its valuation is $1+v_(ℓ)(j-1)$ by the binomial theorem or lifting the exponent. The special value at $j=1$ is zero. The example explains how a single characteristic root can influence many finite levels. A uniform estimate has to track such exceptional behavior.]

Iwasawa theory describes torsion modules by a power of $ℓ$ and distinguished polynomial factors up to finite-module corrections. In the abelian cyclotomic settings used later, the Ferrero–Washington theorem supplies the vanishing of the relevant $mu$-invariant. Roots and exceptional twist indices still require attention. The proof constructs a whole interval of scales on which the exceptional indices that matter are small enough to handle by a rough independent bound.

== A power series tested at many cyclotomic characters <c06s11>

Let $Gamma_0$ be a topologically cyclic group isomorphic to $ℤ_ℓ$ with generator $gamma_0$. Its completed group ring over $ℤ_ℓ$ is obtained by taking compatible finite-level group rings and coefficient quotients. Sending $gamma_0$ to $1+T$ identifies it with $cal("L")=ℤ_ℓ[[T]]$. The quotient by $gamma_0^(ℓ^n)=1$ has the relation $(1+T)^(ℓ^n)-1=0$. A continuous character sending $gamma_0$ to $u in 1+ℓ ℤ_ℓ$ specializes this completed group ring by $T mapsto u-1$. A power series converges at that specialization because the powers of $u-1$ tend to zero.

The simplest nontrivial cyclic torsion module is $cal("L") slash (T-a)$ for $a in ℓ ℤ_ℓ$. Specializing at $T=z$ gives $ℤ_ℓ slash (z-a)$. If $z!=a$ its length is $v_(ℓ)(z-a)$. At $z=a$ the specialization gives an infinite $ℤ_ℓ$-module. In a polynomial with distinct roots in a splitting field, valuations of the differences add, with multiplicities. Thus a single characteristic root can give large but finite lengths at characters very close to it. Vanishing of a different term in a structure theorem will not eliminate this phenomenon.

Take odd $ℓ$, $z_j=(1+ℓ)^j-1$, and $a=z_(j_0)$ for a fixed nonnegative integer $j_0$. If $j!=j_0$, the factor $(1+ℓ)^(j_0)$ in $z_j-a$ is a unit, and the preceding binomial calculation gives
$ v_(ℓ)(z_j-a)=1+v_(ℓ)(j-j_0). $
For $j=j_0+1,dots,j_0+N$, summing the second term counts multiples of $ℓ^r$ for each $r>=1$. Hence
$ sum_(k=1)^N v_(ℓ)(z_(j_0+k)-a)=N+sum_(r>=1) floor(frac(N,ℓ^r))<=N+frac(N,ℓ-1). $
The maximum in this range can grow like $log N$ even though the average stays bounded. A range containing $j_0$ has an infinite term for this toy module. This is a calculation in one cyclic module, not an assertion that any individual arithmetic cohomology group in the proof becomes infinite.

In the structure theory of finitely generated torsion modules over $ℤ_ℓ[[T]]$, the exponent of the factor $ℓ$ in a characteristic ideal is the $mu$-invariant. Other factors are represented by distinguished polynomials, monic polynomials whose lower coefficients are divisible by $ℓ$. A module killed by $ℓ$ can have contributions growing like the order of the finite quotient of $Gamma_0$. A module killed by $T-a$ instead exhibits the near-root behavior just computed. The Ferrero–Washington theorem gives vanishing of the classical $mu$-invariant in the cyclotomic $ℤ_ℓ$-extensions of abelian number fields used as arithmetic inputs. Passing to the particular cohomology modules and varying fields in this proof requires the additional tower estimates in Chapter 16. #link("https://doi.org/10.2307/1971116")[Ferrero and Washington.]

To appreciate the order of choices, imagine that a finite collection of positive integer indices $h$ may each require a separate coarse estimate. A hypothetical bound has two ranges, the small one $h<=x^eta$ and the large one $h>x^C$, with $0<eta<C$. For one fixed $h$ the unsupported values of $x$ satisfy $h^(frac(1,C))<=x<h^(frac(1,eta))$. These intervals depend on the exceptional indices, and those indices can themselves depend on the auxiliary primes. A bound for each fixed twist at all sufficiently large $x$, with a threshold depending on the twist, cannot justify summation over a growing twist range. Chapter 16 uses a uniform bound on the *number* of exceptional indices, combines all of them, then chooses a full interval of $x$ on which the needed estimates hold simultaneously. It is on such an interval that the cohomological lengths can be used in the comparison of integral theta lifts.

== Following one character into the proof <c06s12>

Take a primitive character $psi$ and imagine that its $L$-function has a zero strictly to the right of the critical line. The first question is what real quantity could detect it. Chapters 7–9 use the transforms of Chapter 3. The transform of a ramp centered by its explicit main terms has a pole at the proposed zero, and a high derivative places the mass of an integral inside a chosen interval of $log x$. The analytic consequence is that a certain positive family of real prime sums cannot remain uniformly below a specified power on arbitrarily late windows of prescribed relative width.

Where can an upper bound on that same family come from? Chapter 10 uses the character orthogonality and Frobenius cosets of Chapter 4 to prescribe it in advance on a fixed cyclic quotient. The auxiliary field is allowed to vary while the powers of $psi$ and their coefficients remain fixed. Chapters 11–14 put weights from this family in lattices and theta lifts. The coefficient model and local Fourier calculations in Chapter 5 explain why a map can be divisible at a finite prime, expensive at an infinite place, and comparable to many other maps in a common target. In the actual proof the integral theta kernel and its period give specific estimates on all three quantities.

To use those estimates simultaneously, one needs a bound for extension classes subject to local conditions. Chapters 15–16 use the restriction kernels and tower analysis of the present chapter to control a sum of lengths *on an interval*, even while the field and the twists inside it change. Chapter 17 expands the local prime counts back into the original character family by Fourier inversion. The same centered real statistic then satisfies the arithmetic upper bound that the proposed zero forbade. Chapter 18 chooses the power strictly between the critical line and the real part of that zero and applies the contradiction. The analytic statistic is thus chosen before the varying fields. The interval is determined after the exceptional twists have been counted, so that all its estimates apply simultaneously. #src("Development.AutomorphicArithmetic.CharacterBoxes.ExistsPageN")[Formal statement of the cohomological window.]


